WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:14.849
Natalie Spiska: Perfect. If you could just start by talking about what sort of technology you use in your program right now, whether that be, any wearable technology, stats, as well as, like, trackmans or stuff like that?

2
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:21.599
Coach: So we practice with TrackMan technology, in the winter months, mainly.

3
00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:27.099
Coach: And then we use a STAT program called Circles, which is…

4
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:38.730
Coach: statistical-driven, but it has an AI component to it that will help players develop based on strengths and opportunities. We call them opportunities, not weaknesses, but…

5
00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.500
Coach: You know, the…

6
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.000
Natalie Spiska: Yeah.

7
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.040
Coach: There's a technology component to it,

8
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:55.199
Coach: And… because it's AI-driven, so… which helps us to identify areas where we can work a little bit, and it… I think it speeds up the learning curve on that.

9
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.290
Natalie Spiska: Is that a new program that you've started to implement?

10
00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.350
Coach: We started Circles in the spring of last year.

11
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:12.320
Coach: You know, It's been a bumpy start to getting players to adapt to that.

12
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:13.440
Coach: Yeah.

13
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.649
Coach: There's… it's probably the best…

14
00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:27.570
Coach: system on the market in terms of how easy it is, but, you know, I was trying to be sensitive to how players compete.

15
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:34.639
Coach: and how… You know, there's an element where they have to jot down information, and…

16
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:43.009
Coach: I didn't want it to be a distraction, but at the same time, I feel like it's important so that we can gather information. A lot of times, as you know.

17
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:53.229
Coach: you know, at the end of the round, they say, geez, I didn't putt very well. Well, let's think about that. How many 30-footers did you have? You know, like.

18
00:01:53.770 --> 00:02:11.660
Coach: if you're gonna have 30-footers all day long, you're not gonna putt very well. So, you know, that identifies a ball-striking weakness instead of a putting weakness. So you can adapt using technology that way, and I think it speeds up… speeds up the learning curve.

19
00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:24.130
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, that's… that's super interesting, because I haven't heard of that program yet before, but Clipped is mainly, like, the popular one, and yeah, I definitely understand. Just, like, doing CLIP for a while.

20
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.350
Coach: I'm just gonna interview, sorry.

21
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:34.399
Natalie Spiska: But, if you can also, since I know that there's also a men's team, is there any difference in that technology, or do you guys use the same, technology?

22
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.019
Coach: We use the same technology for both. The men use the stats program

23
00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:45.280
Coach: And they use the, the, pac-Man technology, also.

24
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:51.589
Natalie Spiska: Okay, sounds good. And how has the use of technology in your program changed over the last few years?

25
00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.250
Coach: Definitely… Skyrocketed how much we use it now.

26
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.639
Coach: But I also still think it's very player-driven.

27
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.590
Coach: I, as an instructor, also…

28
00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:15.259
Coach: I think it's important for me to know the information, but not necessarily the player at all times.

29
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.739
Coach: However, players nowadays are much more in tune with

30
00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:26.470
Coach: the numbers, they're just used to practicing on launch monitors, so they know numbers, and…

31
00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:27.060
Natalie Spiska: Yeah.

32
00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:33.029
Coach: Even, like, our players know numbers, they've grown up using technology, so…

33
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:37.289
Coach: We use it sparingly at times, and then…

34
00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.789
Coach: Heavy at other times, depending on what the player's working on.

35
00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:45.479
Coach: A lot of times, I like to hide the numbers completely.

36
00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:49.170
Coach: So that the player doesn't become so number-driven.

37
00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:50.930
Natalie Spiska: Yeah.

38
00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.880
Coach: Ball flight more… Tells the story.

39
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:04.760
Coach: But sometimes you need to know why the ball flight's not doing what you're expecting it to do, and technology really, really helps in that regard.

40
00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:17.329
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, that sort of touches on my next question, which is if there's any particular skill area that you think technology's been most beneficial to use it in. Like, if that's ball striking, putting, short game.

41
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.340
Coach: Definitely ball striking.

42
00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:32.900
Coach: You know, with the ball flight laws, coming into light a lot more, you know, as an instructor, the technology, or the technical information

43
00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:37.989
Coach: Of why the ball curves, and why the ball starts, and…

44
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:46.549
Coach: all that stuff has really skyrocketed over the last couple years, and I feel like… that information…

45
00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.780
Coach: Has come to light by the use of technology.

46
00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.489
Coach: A lot more, and a lot quicker, really quick.

47
00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.749
Coach: And do you think…

48
00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:57.860
Natalie Spiska: Yeah? You can fish it, go ahead.

49
00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:07.750
Coach: The thought is that instruction in the last 10 years has probably gone quicker Than the previous 30 years.

50
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.960
Coach: You know, so that technologies really just help speed up the learning curve on that.

51
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:25.350
Natalie Spiska: And do you think there's a particular, like, personality type of your student athletes that finds technology more beneficial, or do you think it's the same across the board? Like, everyone benefits the same way?

52
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.680
Coach: I think players benefit the same way.

53
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.040
Coach: just different methods.

54
00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:33.720
Natalie Spiska: So…

55
00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.890
Coach: By having the… the ball flight technology,

56
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:46.800
Coach: They're… whether they use it and understand it completely, or they're using the instructor's knowledge.

57
00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.379
Coach: They're using it and getting better quicker.

58
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:51.370
Coach: And…

59
00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.029
Coach: I feel like…

60
00:05:54.270 --> 00:06:12.120
Coach: that nowadays, with players used to the growing up with technology, I feel like they're more in tune with what it is. When it first came out, the Trackman first came out, I think people were intimidated by the fact that the numbers… they didn't understand the numbers, they didn't know…

61
00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.469
Coach: how to utilize the numbers, how to improve quicker. I think that part…

62
00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:22.319
Coach: It was intimidating at first, but now that intimidation factor's gone now.

63
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:30.760
Coach: So I think… I still think the personality of the player If they're more analytical.

64
00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:38.230
Coach: They're going to be wanting to know what the numbers are. If they're more of a field player, they're going to want to see the ball flight curve the way they want to see it.

65
00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:43.840
Coach: But then they might be curious enough to say, okay, now, why is it curving this way?

66
00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:52.250
Coach: The analytical player will want to know every number, every swing, and that gets a little dangerous sometimes.

67
00:06:52.790 --> 00:07:03.979
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, I was gonna ask, do you ever worry that your players are gonna, like, get too lost in the numbers? Because I definitely have been there in the past, where I'm like, I need to just not look at any numbers right now and just swing normally.

68
00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:09.029
Coach: Yeah, I do think that, you know, I grew up as a field player myself.

69
00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:18.099
Coach: So, I think the numbers, although I appreciate the numbers, I would have been a kind of a hide-the-numbers type of player.

70
00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.459
Coach: And use them when I need them. I get worried when a player

71
00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:33.739
Coach: Hits a shot, goes to the monitor, hits a shot, goes to the monitor, and we're constantly, you know, trying to do something. It's…

72
00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:47.249
Coach: I think that that becomes detrimental, like, if… let's use that when we have a problem. If you're seeing the ball the way you want it to see, and you're intending to do this, we don't need to be trying to fix every golf swing.

73
00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:54.939
Coach: And I feel like sometimes the natural feel of a player Gets impacted by too much.

74
00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:10.600
Natalie Spiska: I can, yeah, I can definitely agree with that, where sometimes the ball on screen, the flight is good, and it looks good, but if a certain number's off, you're like, it's not a good shot, when in reality, it is. Like, it'll work on the golf course. No pictures on the scorecard, so…

75
00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.429
Coach: No, and… and to me, the… trying to…

76
00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:23.690
Coach: get the path and the face to zero out. That was a thing there for a while, and I think players have kind of stopped trying to chase that perfect number.

77
00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:29.050
Coach: Because the ball flight, you know, it's not gonna go…

78
00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.240
Coach: Exactly as intended every single time anyway.

79
00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.590
Natalie Spiska: Yeah. And then trying to change the golf swing based on.

80
00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:43.909
Coach: Swing 1 and swing 10, it's a little bit, you can be chasing things that don't need to be chased at that point in time.

81
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.619
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, I've… I can definitely relate to that, for sure.

82
00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:54.919
Natalie Spiska: Are there any technologies that you've tried to use, and then you ended up not using it? And it was… was there any reason for that?

83
00:08:55.890 --> 00:09:04.259
Coach: Not yet. I think that for me, right now, expense on some Things has held us back.

84
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.380
Coach: Yeah. Honestly.

85
00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.149
Coach: Like, would it be… Super interesting to have force plates.

86
00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.429
Coach: On every swing.

87
00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:18.940
Coach: I think it would be, but the expense of having those and how much I would use them…

88
00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:26.319
Coach: Is kind of a… you're at a crossroads there of what technology to introduce at that point.

89
00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:36.529
Coach: But I'm a curious kind of instructor and curious kind of coach in the sense that I want to try to figure out, like, how can I make my players better?

90
00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.440
Coach: And what kind of technology could I… Institute.

91
00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.500
Coach: At the same time, similar to stats.

92
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:51.349
Coach: I feel like sometimes that gets in the way of the player's progress.

93
00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:55.989
Coach: If we're trying to introduce too many things, and the player resists.

94
00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:08.749
Coach: I think we kind of take a step back at that point, too. So I'm careful of how much we introduce and how much we bring into things. I try to go slow and introduce it.

95
00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:10.910
Coach: Not as to distract the player.

96
00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:23.429
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, that's… I feel like that's a good method to employ, just to make sure that, once again, you don't exactly overload, and once again, you're surrounded by all these numbers, and you're like, oh no, everything's broken, when in reality, it's usually not.

97
00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:31.939
Coach: Yeah, and I think that that's something that technology has just made us so much smarter, but at the same time, it…

98
00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:36.870
Coach: you can see the wheels spinning a lot of times, too, and I think that that…

99
00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.999
Coach: Golf is such a feel game, I think, you're better off…

100
00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:46.529
Coach: using it when you need to. And then, you know, you know how it is in the Northeast with…

101
00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:53.890
Coach: trying to get better in the months where you can't get outside, I think that's when it's super important. And I don't think that…

102
00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:57.320
Coach: you know, we're going to [State] tomorrow,

103
00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:01.720
Coach: you know, I don't have portable… technology, so…

104
00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:10.679
Coach: you know, we'll just be seeing the ball go in the air at that point, and I think that all the work that we've done with technology now will help us going forward, so…

105
00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:17.600
Natalie Spiska: Yeah. Are there any types of performance tools or insights that you wish existed but don't yet?

106
00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:24.110
Natalie Spiska: Or, if you can't think of anything, you sort of touched on it a little bit, is there any sort of technology that you wish you could get for your program?

107
00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:33.690
Coach: I do think, more in the terms of putting technology.

108
00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:40.370
Coach: Sam Putt Lab… Putt view, where the…

109
00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:42.180
Coach: the overview.

110
00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:42.820
Natalie Spiska: It goes up and down.

111
00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:46.860
Coach: Yeah, I think that that kind of… technology.

112
00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:51.469
Coach: Into the indoor game, because now the indoor game has so much…

113
00:11:51.630 --> 00:12:01.380
Coach: improved in the last even 3 years compared to what it used to be. And the ability to, you know, we used to just pop up a net in the gymnasium.

114
00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.889
Coach: 10… hit 10, 15 swings, and then switch, you know, and that…

115
00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:19.369
Coach: That now has exploded into a different… everybody has their own bay, everybody has a trackman, and so that kind of technology advancement has really skyrocketed, and…

116
00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:24.719
Coach: I think plotting would be the next thing for me, what we would want to try to

117
00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:27.710
Coach: Yeah, because putting is so important, and…

118
00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:28.450
Natalie Spiska: Yeah.

119
00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:32.679
Coach: And we just don't spend a whole lot of time on that indoors, particularly.

120
00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:39.550
Coach: But, at the same time, putting is a feel aspect that's really feel.

121
00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:49.310
Coach: And technology there could be detrimental, but I do think there's a lot of really good technology that we could use in the short game that we don't currently have.

122
00:12:50.080 --> 00:13:00.590
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, I especially agree with the putting. It's hard to switch from being indoors, especially… we have, like, sort of like a flat green here that can go up and down, but…

123
00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:01.280
Coach: Okay.

124
00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:16.159
Natalie Spiska: it's definitely, like, the green speed's really fast, and we were down in Florida, and the greens are super slow, and suddenly you're like, well, what are we gonna do? We were practicing for fast greens, and now we have slow greens, so…

125
00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.620
Coach: It's always a mixed bag, especially up in the Northeast, I feel like.

126
00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:24.829
Coach: It's, you know, putting from… I… because I think that, you know, even if you're at a

127
00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:30.419
Coach: Place that has fast greens, depending on where your ball is, if you're above the hole, below the hole…

128
00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.369
Coach: You've got curvature, you've got length…

129
00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:34.300
Natalie Spiska: Yep.

130
00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:42.270
Coach: every green's really different, so you have to be able to adapt, and I think that that's where technology could speed that up a little bit.

131
00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:49.089
Coach: Where you, on one point, you could hit a really severe uphill putt and a really severe downhill putt.

132
00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:54.020
Coach: And be able to quickly learn The stroke tendencies on that.

133
00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:59.700
Coach: So I do think that there's technology In the short game that…

134
00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:03.590
Coach: we don't currently utilize that I think would be hugely beneficial.

135
00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:17.099
Natalie Spiska: I just have a few more questions for you. What role do you think technology will play in college golf in the next 5 to 10 years, and if you think that'll impact the recruiting process in any way?

136
00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:22.620
Coach: I think it's going to go light speed, yes.

137
00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:26.370
Coach: It's going to go crazy, because now…

138
00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:30.370
Coach: You're really seeing young players that we're recruiting now

139
00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:39.090
Coach: that every day are using technology. So this… and I think that that's going to impact recruiting, because

140
00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:42.400
Coach: If they're using it in their own game and we don't have it.

141
00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.869
Coach: That might cause a player to go to a different school if…

142
00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:51.300
Coach: we don't have what they're already used to having.

143
00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:56.859
Coach: And you can see that with some players now, and I think COVID changed that, where…

144
00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:00.070
Coach: The… the players now that are…

145
00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:11.100
Coach: freshmen, seniors in high school, juniors in high school, all grew up using technology, so I… to answer that, and I… it's going to go…

146
00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:16.340
Coach: Light speed fast. And we're gonna, we're gonna have to really adapt.

147
00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:18.410
Natalie Spiska: Yeah, I've…

148
00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:34.879
Natalie Spiska: I've spoken to a few other coaches, and they've all agreed with that as well, where in the recruiting process especially, all of these young golfers that are coming through are so much more used to technology, and they're using it in their everyday. So, yeah, it'll be interesting to see where it ends up in a few years even.

149
00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:38.229
Coach: The next thing that I'm interested in using

150
00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:46.239
Coach: technology for, too, that I think is going to be part of how fast this goes is physical fitness.

151
00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:47.410
Natalie Spiska: Hmm, yeah.

152
00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:52.370
Coach: And how players adapt to exercise and workouts.

153
00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:56.699
Coach: and using… AI is a little intimidating.

154
00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:03.350
Coach: But I do think that there's a lot of… instances where AI…

155
00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:11.459
Coach: Is going to really change how players work out, how they… how their body feels after an event,

156
00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:14.120
Coach: And then being able to recover quickly.

157
00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:20.909
Coach: And then… used… Things like technology to get better speed-wise.

158
00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:22.300
Natalie Spiska: Hmm.

159
00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.659
Coach: We're seeing it a little bit, and I… I don't know…

160
00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:32.009
Coach: If it would be considered technology, but, we use Golf Forever.

161
00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:36.180
Coach: Which is the program that Scotty Scheffler uses to warm up.

162
00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.240
Natalie Spiska: Hm, yes. Okay.

163
00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.600
Coach: It's the… it's resistance bands, it's stretching,

164
00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:48.790
Coach: And we utilize videos, but it can be tailored from player to player based on their physical fitness ability.

165
00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:55.249
Coach: So that's where I think technology… That's going to explode.

166
00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:58.930
Coach: to where… The individual workouts.

167
00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:00.020
Natalie Spiska: Yeah?

168
00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:08.369
Coach: The individual workout's going to be tailored exactly to what you need, whether it's hip mobility, ankle mobility, core strength.

169
00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:11.030
Coach: That's gonna be driven directly to you.

170
00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:16.600
Coach: So you can watch a 15-minute video, and do a workout in your dorm room, and…

171
00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:25.759
Coach: you know, that's gonna just be amazing. I think players are gonna be way more ready to play high-level tournament golf because of that.

172
00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:28.019
Natalie Spiska: Yeah.

173
00:17:28.249 --> 00:17:39.410
Natalie Spiska: especially with the AI analysis system that you mentioned, like, how would that work in, like, a fitness setting? Like, it analyzes you, and it tells you if your form is correct, or… is that sort of it?

174
00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:48.119
Coach: I think it's… I think the… the canvas is clear. I think what you can do with AI in terms of…

175
00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:50.540
Coach: Not only just answering

176
00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:59.899
Coach: But then maybe even being able to physically watch a movement, whether it's, you know, watching a golf swing and then saying, wow, you know.

177
00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:04.899
Coach: And… To a certain extent, when you look at video now, you can…

178
00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:08.149
Coach: You know, how much of the hip turn is…

179
00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:16.750
Coach: being utilized? How much of a sway is going back and forth? You… you can already see some of that stuff,

180
00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:27.940
